WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
The first film traced inherited information to DNA.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.600
How can DNA make a copy?

3
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.940
The clue is not the helix shape by itself.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.620
It is the matching rule inside a duplex.

5
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.160
We will carry the first film's blue DNA into a picture made only of labelled blocks.

6
00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:28.983
Start with base composition, the percentages of adenine, thymine, guanine and

7
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:35.383
cytosine. Across double-stranded samples, adenine lies close to thymine, while

8
00:00:35.383 --> 00:00:37.243
guanine lies close to cytosine.

9
00:00:38.423 --> 00:00:42.983
A model must explain both matches while allowing different samples to carry

10
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.103
different base sequences.

11
00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:49.497
Place two blue rails side by side, running in opposite directions.

12
00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:55.897
In canonical Watson-Crick pairing, adenine pairs with thymine, and guanine pairs

13
00:00:55.897 --> 00:01:00.597
with cytosine. The paired blocks explain the whole-duplex ratios.

14
00:01:01.957 --> 00:01:04.757
The rails are schematic, not molecular structures.

15
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:10.110
Now unzip one short rail, leaving its partner positions empty.

16
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:12.890
The old blocks remain blue.

17
00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.210
New blocks will arrive with green outlines.

18
00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:20.410
Predict the new partner for the sequence shown on screen.

19
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.690
Remember, adenine selects thymine, and guanine selects cytosine.

20
00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.430
Which blocks fill the empty rail?

21
00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:37.257
Each position admits its complementary block.

22
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:42.137
The second rail is therefore specified by the first, not guessed.

23
00:01:43.477 --> 00:01:47.417
Reverse the reading direction, because the two strands are antiparallel.

24
00:01:48.797 --> 00:01:52.197
When both old rails separate, each can guide one new partner.

25
00:01:53.557 --> 00:01:56.697
One duplex can therefore become two matching duplexes.

26
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.900
That is a copying proposal, not yet an experiment.

27
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.120
It predicts that every daughter duplex keeps one old strand and receives one new

28
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.520
strand. Give old strands a heavy marker and new strands a light marker.

29
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.180
Their combined density can reveal which strands stay together.

30
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.330
Before transfer, DNA forms a heavy band.

31
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.810
After one generation in light nitrogen, every duplex should contain one heavy and

32
00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.570
one light strand. That gives one hybrid band.

33
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:38.050
After another generation, half the duplexes remain hybrid, while half contain two

34
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.050
light strands.

35
00:02:39.833 --> 00:02:44.613
If parental strands stayed together, separate heavy and light bands would appear

36
00:02:44.613 --> 00:02:45.893
after the first generation.

37
00:02:47.253 --> 00:02:51.733
If old material dispersed through both strands, one band would shift gradually.

38
00:02:53.093 --> 00:02:57.693
The observed hybrid band, followed by hybrid and light bands, matched strand

39
00:02:57.693 --> 00:03:02.213
retention. The pattern, not exact density, is the test.

40
00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:05.273
Pairing does two jobs in this model.

41
00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:11.773
It explains why complementary bases match across a duplex, and it lets each old

42
00:03:11.773 --> 00:03:13.633
strand specify a new sequence.

43
00:03:14.953 --> 00:03:18.853
The density bands test whether each daughter keeps one parental strand.

44
00:03:20.073 --> 00:03:22.273
They do not test every enzyme step.

45
00:03:23.693 --> 00:03:26.873
Structure supplied a rule; experiment tested its consequence.
